From c559d180ad21e4ab8e11d066d1bb121143804da2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tserdeiro <tserdeiro@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 05:54:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(git): scope auto-commit's git add to what the command
 produced

auto-commit.sh (and its PowerShell and Python twins) stage the working
tree with a blanket `git add .` before committing. This sweeps in every
untracked file anywhere in the project, not just what the triggering
command actually produced -- an in-progress edit in an unrelated file,
directory, or feature gets swept into the same auto-commit.

Stage only what the event's own command is responsible for:
.specify/memory/ for after_constitution; the whole working tree
(git add -A, same as before this scoping) for before_implement and
after_implement, because implementation code lands wherever the project
puts it -- src/, lib/, anywhere -- never only inside the feature
directory, so these two events cannot be scoped to it; the active
feature's directory (specs/<feature>/) for every other event, resolved
the same way every other script resolves it (SPECIFY_FEATURE_DIRECTORY,
else .specify/feature.json). When no feature directory can be resolved
-- no active feature yet, or the core scripts aren't installed -- fall
back to `git add -u`, so an in-progress edit to an already-tracked file
still commits without ever adding an untracked file outside a known
scope. The existing "no changes to commit" check keeps its whole-repo
reach; a new check after staging catches the case where that broader
check finds pending changes but the event's own scope holds none,
skipping cleanly instead of committing an empty index.

Existing tests that exercised the blanket `add .` behavior now write
their fixture's changed file under a resolvable feature directory; new
tests cover every staging path -- the feature directory and the
constitution directory (an unrelated untracked file elsewhere is never
staged), the tracked-only fallback, and before_implement/after_implement
(a source file outside the feature directory is committed, proving the
repo-wide stage still captures real implementation changes).
